WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.235
What if the highest mountains on Earth

2
00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.512
began as mud at the bottom of

3
00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:04.546
a sea?

4
00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:06.076
The Himalayas did.

5
00:00:06.476 --> 00:00:08.078
About two hundred million years ago,

6
00:00:08.078 --> 00:00:10.203
India began to drift north.

7
00:00:10.203 --> 00:00:12.920
Between it and Asia lay a sea,

8
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.300
the Tethys, slowly filling with layers of

9
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.389
sediment.

10
00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:19.123
Squeeze soft layers from two sides and

11
00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:20.841
they bend, like a rug pushed against

12
00:00:20.841 --> 00:00:21.677
a wall.

13
00:00:21.677 --> 00:00:23.637
Geographers call that folding.

14
00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:26.522
About forty to fifty million years ago,

15
00:00:26.522 --> 00:00:28.553
India ran into Asia.

16
00:00:28.553 --> 00:00:30.690
Both plates were too light to sink,

17
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.393
so the crust between them crumpled upward.

18
00:00:33.793 --> 00:00:35.558
The upfolds are called anticlines,

19
00:00:35.558 --> 00:00:37.798
and the downfolds are synclines.

20
00:00:37.798 --> 00:00:40.016
Together they make the long parallel ranges

21
00:00:40.016 --> 00:00:41.802
we see in the Himalayas.

22
00:00:42.202 --> 00:00:44.025
The push has not stopped.

23
00:00:44.025 --> 00:00:45.952
The Himalayas still rise by more than

24
00:00:45.952 --> 00:00:47.682
a centimetre a year,

25
00:00:47.682 --> 00:00:49.783
and their rocks still carry fossils from

26
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.000
that old sea.

27
00:00:51.401 --> 00:00:52.864
For GS One:

28
00:00:52.864 --> 00:00:54.420
fold mountains are young,

29
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.254
formed where plates converge,

30
00:00:56.254 --> 00:00:58.853
and the Himalayas are the textbook case.

